Vocal cord nodules
Opthalmology & ENT
Chronic hoarseness/vocal fatigue, Bilateral symmetric cord swellings, History of voice abuse

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychological distress and functional panic due to complete voice failure the night before a major professional engagement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a professional singer/teacher who experienced an acute, distressing loss of functional vocal range and severe hoarseness leading to panic. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out acute epiglottitis, an acute vocal cord hemorrhage, or an expanding neck mass, and to provide immediate clinical reassurance, safety-netting, and strict behavioral guidance to prevent irreversible laryngeal damage before their event tomorrow.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My voice just completely cracked and vanished during rehearsals. My throat feels incredibly tired, and I cannot force a sound out without it breaking."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a chronic, progressive, and unremitting hoarseness, breathiness, and a distinctly raspy voice quality over the last 6 months. Endorses frequent, debilitating vocal fatigue that noticeably worsens progressively toward the end of the day. Tonight, experienced acute pitch breaks, noticeably reduced functional vocal range, and a distinct inability to sing or speak loudly. Strong, explicit history of chronic phonotrauma and demanding professional voice use (school teacher and choir singer). Notes a constant subjective sensation of a lump and excessive thick mucus, causing constant throat clearing. Denies fever, stridor, odynophagia, or difficulty breathing.
Functional Status: Highly anxious, speaking in a strained, forced whisper.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe occupational anxiety regarding their inability to perform tomorrow.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, extremely anxious, demonstrating obvious physical strain in the neck muscles when attempting to phonate.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam is completely normal; no erythema, exudate, or tonsillar enlargement. Neck palpation reveals no cervical lymphadenopathy, no midline masses, and the thyroid is normal. Laryngeal palpation elicits no acute tenderness, and the trachea is midline. Chest auscultation is perfectly clear. Absolutely no stridor or signs of acute airway obstruction. (Note: Definitive diagnosis of bilateral, symmetrical, firm, pinpoint swellings located at the junction of the anterior and middle thirds of the true vocal cords requires formal videostroboscopy, but clinical history is pathognomonic).
Environmental Observations: Multiple herbal teas and lozenge wrappers scattered around the room.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications. Has been overusing menthol lozenges, which dry the mucosa.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Vocal Cord Nodules - Status: Acute vocal fatigue/failure
Differentials:

Acute Laryngitis/Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absolute absence of systemic inflammatory symptoms, high fever, severe odynophagia, or acute infectious prodrome; the pathology is strictly mechanical and chronic.

Laryngeal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific symptom trigger (prolonged voice abuse in a professional setting) and absence of massive fixed cervical lymphadenopathy or constitutional symptoms (weight loss), though formal scoping is required.

Vocal Cord Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, progressive nature of the hoarseness over 6 months, rather than a singular, sudden, sharp pain and instant voice loss following a single scream.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Severe occupational and psychological distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute occupational panic driven by the functional failure of a chronically abused larynx. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was strictly necessary to definitively exclude an acute infectious airway emergency (epiglottitis) or a sudden traumatic cord hemorrhage, both of which require entirely different emergent pathways. By ruling out acute airway compromise, the visit safely localized the pathology to chronic mechanical phonotrauma (nodules). The critical intervention was not pharmacological, but rather the strict imposition of enforced voice rest and psychological stabilization, preventing the patient from forcing their voice and causing permanent fibrotic damage prior to formal ENT evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Imposed an absolute, non-negotiable medical mandate for strict vocal rest for the next 48 hours (including cancellation of the professional event). Generated a routine referral to an ENT specialist for rigid or flexible videostroboscopy and subsequent targeted speech pathology.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent further mucosal damage and reverse the drying effect of constant throat clearing, the patient must take a small sip of room-temperature water every single time they feel the urge to clear their throat. The physical act of swallowing washes the mucus away and hydrates the cords without violently slamming them together (positive reinforcement through relief of the lump sensation), systematically replacing the destructive habit of chronic throat clearing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Must attend a GP review in 7 days to organize the ENT and speech pathology referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient suddenly develops noisy breathing (stridor), severe shortness of breath, or an absolute inability to swallow their own saliva.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Definitive diagnosis of nodules requires a camera test (laryngoscopy) by an ENT specialist.
Clinical Course: Your voice will functionally improve over the next 48 hours with strict silence. However, the underlying calluses on your vocal cords will not disappear without long-term vocal therapy and modified speaking techniques.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you suddenly find it hard to breathe, or if you start making a loud whistling sound when drawing breath in, go to the emergency department.
